@database "llm_ollama"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_ollama.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Ollama provider"
@{h1}Ollama provider

This plugin lets agents use models served by Ollama -- on this machine or on
another one in the network. It is an LLM provider plugin: it has no tools, no
hooks and no panel. You use it by writing @{tt}provider: ollama@{utt} in a
model entry of @{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt}; agents reach that entry through a
profile.

An entry runs in one of two modes, chosen by @{tt}ollama_mode@{utt}:

@{table}
ollama_mode | Endpoint | Client
native | POST <base_url>/api/chat, streamed as NDJSON | this plugin's own httpx client
openai_compat (the default) | POST <base_url>/chat/completions of Ollama's OpenAI-compatible endpoint | the client of @{" llm_openai " link llm_openai/main}, borrowed through the registry
@{body}

Use @{b}native@{ub}. It is the only mode that sends the context size, the
thinking switch and the output cap in Ollama's own terms (see
@{" The two modes " link modes}).

How models, profiles and fallback chains work in general is in
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}; this manual covers what
this provider does with an entry.

@{code}
For users (the operator writing llm.yaml)
  @{"  The two modes  " link modes}
  @{"  Model entry keys  " link keys}
  @{"  Context size and memory  " link context}
  @{"  Thinking  " link thinking}
  @{"  Timeouts, streaming and retries  " link retries}
  @{"  Usage, cost and the hooks  " link usage}
  @{"  Errors  " link errors}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node modes "The two modes"
@{h1}The two modes

@{h2}native

The plugin's own client talks to Ollama's @{tt}/api/chat@{utt}. The
@{tt}base_url@{utt} is the server root, without @{tt}/v1@{utt}; unset, it is
@{tt}http://127.0.0.1:11434@{utt}. Everything else in this manual describes
this mode.

@{h2}openai_compat

Nothing of this plugin's client is used. The entry is copied with
@{tt}provider: openai@{utt} and handed to the @{tt}openai@{utt} provider of
@{" llm_openai " link llm_openai/main}, which then does all the work: the
request, its retries, its errors and its reports to the hooks are those
described in @{" Retries " link llm_openai/retries},
@{" Errors " link llm_openai/errors} and
@{" Usage, cost and the hooks " link llm_openai/usage}. What this plugin
changes on the copy:

@{bullet}@{tt}base_url@{utt}: as set, else
@{tt}http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1@{utt} -- here the path must end in
@{tt}/v1@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}api_key@{utt}: as set, else the word @{tt}ollama@{utt}; Ollama
checks none.
@{bullet}@{tt}thinking_level@{utt}, @{tt}thinking_budget@{utt},
@{tt}include_thoughts@{utt} and @{tt}prompt_cache_key@{utt} are removed with
a warning; @{tt}modalities@{utt} and @{tt}prompt_cache_mode@{utt} are removed
silently. The dialect keys (@{tt}reasoning_details_mode@{utt},
@{tt}tool_schema_dialect@{utt}, ...) are warned about once, as not wired for
provider=ollama, and removed.

The price of this mode: @{tt}context_window@{utt} is not sent, so the model
loads with the Ollama server's own default context length, whatever the entry
says (see @{" Context size and memory " link context}); there is no thinking
switch. The mode needs the llm_openai plugin and the @{tt}openai@{utt}
package.
@endnode

@node keys "Model entry keys"
@{h1}Model entry keys

The keys a @{b}native@{ub} entry reads. Everything else in
@{tt}LLMModelConfig@{utt} is either handled by the core (@{tt}extends@{utt},
profiles) or not read here.

@{table}
Key | Default | What the client does with it
provider | -- | ollama
ollama_mode | openai_compat | native selects this client
model | -- | the model name as Ollama knows it (@{tt}gemma4:12b@{utt})
base_url | http://127.0.0.1:11434 | the server root; requests go to @{tt}/api/chat@{utt}
context_window | 32768 | sent as @{tt}options.num_ctx@{utt} on every request; 0 sends nothing. See @{" Context size and memory " link context}
temperature | none | sent as @{tt}options.temperature@{utt}
max_tokens | none | sent as @{tt}options.num_predict@{utt}; 0 or unset sends nothing
request_timeout | 120 | seconds, see @{" Timeouts, streaming and retries " link retries}
thinking_level | none | sent as @{tt}think@{utt}, see @{" Thinking " link thinking}
include_thoughts | none | sent as @{tt}think@{utt} (true/false) when thinking_level is unset
reasoning_details_mode | keep_all | which earlier thinking goes back to the model: keep_all, keep_last, strip
capabilities | all defaults | streaming decides the path; structured_output allows response formats; image_input and audio_input shape tool attachments; developer_role; default_api_type must be unset or chat_completions (anything else fails when the client is built)
@{body}

@{tt}thinking_budget@{utt} has no Ollama counterpart: it is ignored with a
warning. The other dialect keys (@{tt}tool_schema_dialect@{utt},
@{tt}assistant_reasoning_field@{utt}, @{tt}thinking_request_shape@{utt},
@{tt}stream_silence_timeout@{utt}, @{tt}provider_affinity_minutes@{utt}) are
logged at build time as "not wired ... will be ignored". Not read at all:
@{tt}api_key@{utt} (no key is sent), @{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt},
@{tt}modalities@{utt}, the prompt-cache keys.

TLS verification of an @{tt}https://@{utt} base URL follows the app's setting
(@{tt}network.ssl_verify@{utt}).

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  models:
    ollama-gemma-4:
      provider: ollama
      model: gemma4:12b
      base_url: http://127.0.0.1:11434
      ollama_mode: native
      context_window: 32768
      request_timeout: 240
      capabilities:
        tools: true
        function_calling: true
        streaming: true
        image_input: true
@{body}
@endnode

@node context "Context size and memory"
@{h1}Context size and memory

In native mode @{tt}context_window@{utt} is sent as @{tt}num_ctx@{utt} with
every request -- 32768 when the entry does not set it. The same number is
the window the chat's context figures are measured against, so what the chat
shows and what the model holds agree.

For Ollama the context is memory: it reserves room for @{tt}num_ctx@{utt}
tokens when it loads the model. A context that does not fit into the
graphics card next to the model's weights makes Ollama run part of the model
on the CPU, which is many times slower. Measured on a local setup: a 256k
context pushed an 8B model onto the CPU. Set @{tt}context_window@{utt} to
what the card holds, not to the most the model can take; @{tt}ollama ps@{utt}
on the server shows how a loaded model is split between CPU and GPU.

In openai_compat mode none of this applies: the context is the server's
default, and the entry cannot change it.
@endnode

@node thinking "Thinking"
@{h1}Thinking

@{h2}Switching it

Ollama's @{tt}think@{utt} field is a switch or a level. The entry sets it
through @{tt}thinking_level@{utt}:

@{table}
thinking_level | think
none | false
minimal, low | "low"
medium | "medium"
high, xhigh, max | "high"
@{body}

Without @{tt}thinking_level@{utt}, @{tt}include_thoughts: true/false@{utt} is
sent as it is; with neither, @{tt}think@{utt} is not sent and the model
decides -- a thinking model then thinks. Whether a model honours
@{tt}think: false@{utt} is up to the model.

Ollama refuses @{tt}think@{utt} (true or a level) with a 400 for a model that
cannot think. So before the first request that would send it, the client asks
@{tt}/api/show@{utt} once (at most 10 s) whether the model has the thinking
capability. If not, @{tt}think@{utt} is left out of every request, with one
warning in the log. If the question fails, @{tt}think@{utt} is sent as
configured. @{tt}false@{utt} is always sent without asking.

@{h2}What happens to the thinking

@{bullet}It streams as thinking deltas, which the chat shows and the
reasoning-loop watchdog reads, and is kept on the answer as
@{tt}reasoning_content@{utt}.
@{bullet}It goes back to the model in the @{tt}thinking@{utt} field of the
assistant message it belongs to, as far as @{tt}reasoning_details_mode@{utt}
allows: @{tt}keep_all@{utt} (default) every earlier turn's,
@{tt}keep_last@{utt} only the latest, @{tt}strip@{utt} none. The model's
template decides how much of it it renders.
@{bullet}Its tokens are part of @{tt}completion_tokens@{utt}; Ollama counts
them apart nowhere.
@endnode

@node retries "Timeouts, streaming and retries"
@{h1}Timeouts, streaming and retries

@{h2}request_timeout

One number, 120 s unless the entry sets it. It is httpx's timeout for every
phase of a request (connect, read, write, pool) and, while streaming, the
longest gap between two lines. A request that finds the model unloaded
waits for the server to load it, so keep the timeout above that load
time.

@{h2}Streaming or not

A model streams unless its entry says @{tt}capabilities.streaming:
false@{utt}. Ollama streams one JSON object per line; text, thinking and
tool calls are passed on as they arrive (a tool call comes whole, not in
pieces). The last line carries the finish reason (@{tt}done_reason@{utt}:
@{tt}stop@{utt}, @{tt}length@{utt} when @{tt}num_predict@{utt} cut the
answer) and the token counts.

@{h2}Retries

Only the streaming path retries, and only these; the numbers are fixed in the
code:

@{table}
What happened | What the client does
HTTP 5xx | retry, then LLMServerError
dropped connection, network or protocol error | retry, then LLMConnectionError
timeout, or no line for request_timeout | no retry: LLMConnectionError at once
HTTP 429 | no retry: LLMRateLimitError at once
any other status (400, 404, ...) | no retry: httpx.HTTPStatusError
@{body}

Up to three retries (four attempts), after 1, 2 and 4 s. Every retry is
reported to the post_llm_response hooks as its own row
(@{tt}[RETRY n/4] Server error (503)@{utt},
@{tt}[RETRY n/4] Stream interrupted: ...@{utt}; finish reason
@{tt}retry@{utt}) and as a status line in the chat. A cancelled run
interrupts every wait.

A retry starts from scratch: when the caller has already received deltas of
the failed attempt, the stream first sends one
@{tt}{"type": "stream_restart"}@{utt} chunk, and the chat drops what it has
shown of that attempt.

The non-streaming path sends once; a failure is raised as the same types at
once. Where the client stops, the agent server's fallback chain
(@{tt}llm_profile@{utt} with more than one profile) takes over; see
@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}.

@{h2}Cancel

A cancelled run stops before the client reads the next line, and a blocking
request is abandoned at once; the cancel reaches the agent as a cancel, never
as an answer. A caller that stops reading a stream closes its HTTP connection
at once.
@endnode

@node usage "Usage, cost and the hooks"
@{h1}Usage, cost and the hooks

Each request is reported to the @{tt}pre_llm_request@{utt} hooks before it is
sent, with the full request body, and to the @{tt}post_llm_response@{utt}
hooks when it ends -- exactly once, however it ends. The message debugger,
the OpenTelemetry chat span and the cost figures are built from these reports
(@{" Hooks " link scarabhive/hooks},
@{" The capture hooks " link message_debugger/hooks},
@{" What gets exported " link otel/spans},
@{" What it records " link context_usage_tracker/hooks}).

@{h2}One report per ending

@{table}
The request ended with | post_llm_response carries
an answer | usage and finish reason; a blocking request also the whole response body
a retry | an error @{tt}[RETRY n/4] ...@{utt}, finish reason retry
a refusal | the error of the exception raised: @{tt}Ollama 404: model 'x' not found@{utt} (Ollama's own reason), @{tt}Network/protocol error: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Stream failed after 4 attempts: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Ollama answered 200 with a body that is no JSON: ...@{utt}
an error line in the stream | @{tt}Ollama: <reason>@{utt}
a cancel before it was sent, or while it was out | @{tt}Request cancelled by user@{utt} (@{tt}cancelled@{utt} when the run's task was cancelled)
a cancel during a retry wait | @{tt}Request cancelled during retry wait@{utt}, after the retry's row
a stream the caller stopped reading | @{tt}stream abandoned by the caller@{utt}; when the agent stopped it for a reasoning loop, that reason and finish reason @{tt}reasoning_loop_aborted@{utt} instead
anything unexpected | the exception's text
@{body}

@{h2}The usage

@{tt}prompt_eval_count@{utt} becomes @{tt}prompt_tokens@{utt},
@{tt}eval_count@{utt} @{tt}completion_tokens@{utt} (thinking included), and
their sum @{tt}total_tokens@{utt}. Ollama reports no cached tokens. A stream
carries its counts only on its last line, so a cancelled or abandoned stream
is reported without usage.

Ollama bills nothing, so the usage carries @{tt}cost: 0@{utt}, and no model
needs a row in @{tt}config/llm_pricing.yaml@{utt}. In openai_compat mode
the openai client reports the usage, without a cost: the core prices the
tokens from that file, and a model without a row is reported as unknown.
@endnode

@node errors "Errors"
@{h1}Errors

What reaches the agent server when a request cannot be answered. The server
decides from the type whether to fall back to the next profile of the chain,
and whether to block the model for a while
(@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}).

@{table}
Raised | When
httpx.HTTPStatusError | a 4xx other than 429, at once. The message carries Ollama's reason: @{tt}Ollama 404: model 'x' not found@{utt}, @{tt}Ollama 400: ... does not support tools@{utt}
LLMRateLimitError | a 429, at once
LLMServerError | a 5xx (streaming: after the retries); @{tt}Ollama 503: ...@{utt}
LLMConnectionError | the server is unreachable, times out, or the stream stalls; streaming: a dropped stream after the retries, or a stream that ends without Ollama's closing @{tt}done@{utt} line
LLMConnectionError | an answer that is no JSON -- a proxy, a captive portal or a web UI answered instead of Ollama: @{tt}Ollama answered 200 with a body that is no JSON: <first 200 characters>@{utt}
asyncio.CancelledError | the run was cancelled
NotImplementedError at build time | capabilities.default_api_type is something other than chat_completions
@{body}

@{h2}What the agent server does with them

@{table}
Failure | Fallback | Block of the model
5xx | next profile at once | none
timeout, unreachable, no JSON | next profile at once | none
404 (also 401, 402, 403, e.g. behind an auth proxy) | next profile at once | for the agent's @{tt}fallback_recovery_seconds@{utt} (default 3600 s), for every agent
400 (context too long, no tool support, ...) | next profile at once | none
429 | next profile at once | a short rate-limit block that grows while it repeats
@{body}

A block holds for this model on this @{tt}base_url@{utt} only: the same
model name on another Ollama host stays available. Until 30.09.2026 a
streamed failure came as an error answer instead -- the server asked the same
model once more before it fell back, and blocked nothing.

@{h2}Errors that come as an answer

When the stream has started (HTTP 200) and then sends an error line -- the
model runner crashed or ran out of memory -- the answer is empty with an
@{tt}error@{utt} of type @{tt}ollama_api_error@{utt} on the assistant
message. The agent server asks the model once more, then falls back.

@{h2}chat()

The plain text call (used by summaries, routers and other one-shot requests)
goes through the blocking path, reports like it and returns the answer's
text; the exceptions above are raised as they are.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}Messages

@{bullet}Roles are @{tt}system@{utt}, @{tt}user@{utt}, @{tt}assistant@{utt}
and @{tt}tool@{utt}; a tool result names its tool in @{tt}tool_name@{utt}.
Internal bookkeeping fields are removed.
@{bullet}Developer notes become system messages -- Ollama knows no other
role, and a role the chat template does not know would be dropped
silently. An entry whose template renders only the first system message
sets @{tt}capabilities.developer_role: user@{utt}. A note that ends the
request always goes as a user message wrapped in note tags: a request that
ends on a system message asks for nothing and gets nothing.
@{bullet}Content is plain text. Images go in the message's
@{tt}images@{utt} field as base64 -- from data URLs and base64 image parts;
an image given as a web URL is dropped. Text files become
@{tt}[File: name]@{utt} followed by their text. Audio and video are dropped.
@{bullet}Images a tool returns follow the tool result as a user message; a
model without @{tt}image_input@{utt} gets a text note in their place.
@{bullet}Tool call arguments go back as objects, as Ollama expects; a stored
argument string that is no valid JSON is repaired if possible, else sent as
@{tt}{}@{utt}.

@{h2}Tools

Tool schemas go out in @{tt}tools@{utt} as the agent built them; there is no
@{tt}tool_choice@{utt}. A model without tool support answers with a 400
("does not support tools"), raised as described in
@{" Errors " link errors}. Tool calls without an id get one
(@{tt}call_<id>@{utt}).

@{h2}Structured output

A response format goes in Ollama's @{tt}format@{utt} field: the word
@{tt}json@{utt} for JSON mode, the schema itself for a JSON schema. The
entry must declare @{tt}capabilities.structured_output@{utt}; a call asking
for a format it does not declare is refused rather than answered with free
text.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing to enable: the core finds LLM provider plugins by their
@{tt}plugin.toml@{utt} (@{tt}type = ["llm-provider"]@{utt}) and loads
@{tt}provider.py@{utt} when a model entry names one of its providers. There
is no entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} and no allowlist line.

@{table}
Manifest key | Names
provides | ollama
@{body}

@{h2}Dependencies

None of its own: native mode uses httpx, which the core requires.
openai_compat mode needs the llm_openai plugin and its @{tt}openai@{utt}
package.

@{h2}The server

An Ollama server reachable at @{tt}base_url@{utt}, with the model pulled
(@{tt}ollama pull <model>@{utt}); a model it does not have answers 404. A
server on another machine must listen on the network, not only on
localhost.

@{h2}Where things live

Nothing on disk. Changes to @{tt}llm.yaml@{utt} reach an agent when it
builds its client, i.e. after a restart.
@endnode
